# PostgreSQL connection. TLS for remote hosts is applied automatically (below).
#
# This dashboard runs on Supabase. Take the string from the project's
# Connect dialog and use a SESSION-mode connection, not the transaction pooler:
#
#   direct          postgresql://postgres:PASSWORD@db.PROJECT_REF.supabase.co:5432/postgres
#   session pooler  postgresql://postgres.PROJECT_REF:PASSWORD@aws-0-eu-west-1.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres
#
# Note the username on the pooler: postgres.PROJECT_REF, not postgres. That is
# how the pooler picks the project, and a plain "postgres" cannot authenticate.
# Copy the URI from the project's Connect dialog rather than assembling it: the
# region prefix (aws-0 / aws-1) varies too. The boot log prints the user and host
# it is using (never the password), so a mismatch is one glance away.
#
# The pooler strips the .PROJECT_REF suffix and authenticates upstream as
# `postgres`, so BOTH a missing ref and a wrong password fail with the identical
# "password authentication failed for user postgres". Check the username on the
# boot log line first; if it already carries the ref, the password is the
# problem. A password with @ : / ? # % or & in it must be percent-encoded to
# survive the URI.
#
# Port 6543 (the transaction pooler) will NOT work. The migration runner takes a
# session-level pg_advisory_lock and the migrations define their guard helpers in
# pg_temp, and both are tied to one session: behind a transaction-mode pooler the
# next statement can land on a different connection and the run fails, or worse
# half-applies. The session pooler on 5432 is the one to use if the host has no
# IPv6 (Render does not), the direct host otherwise.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.PROJECT_REF:PASSWORD@aws-0-eu-west-1.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres

# TLS: every non-local host is reached over TLS whatever the URL says
# (server/src/lib/db-ssl.js). The certificate is verified when PGSSLROOTCERT
# names the provider's CA bundle (e.g. Supabase's), when the URL says
# sslmode=verify-full, or with DATABASE_SSL_VERIFY=true (system trust store);
# otherwise the connection is encrypted without verifying the certificate.
#
# DO NOT set DATABASE_SSL_VERIFY=true on its own against Supabase. It verifies
# against the system trust store, and Supabase's pooler presents its own CA,
# which is not in it — the server then fails to start with "self-signed
# certificate in certificate chain" (the migration runner connects first, so it
# looks like a migration failure). To verify a Supabase connection, download the
# project's CA bundle (Supabase → Settings → Database → SSL configuration), keep
# it in the repository — it is a public certificate — and point PGSSLROOTCERT at
# it. PGSSLROOTCERT alone turns verification on; DATABASE_SSL_VERIFY is then
# redundant. With neither, the connection is still encrypted, just unverified,
# which is what the core API does.
# PGSSLROOTCERT=/opt/render/project/src/db/supabase-ca.crt
# DATABASE_SSL_VERIFY=true
# Only for a private network that cannot offer TLS (logged at every start):
# DATABASE_SSL=disable

# The share of Supabase's connection budget this service may hold. Default 5.
#
# Session mode caps the whole PROJECT at pool_size clients (15 by default),
# shared by every service pointed at the database -- here, this dashboard and
# the email bot, whose own pool is MAX_DB_CONNECTIONS (5).
#
# Count a deploy, not a quiet afternoon: a zero-downtime host keeps the old
# instance serving until the new one is healthy, so two copies of this service
# draw on the budget at once. Three, three and the bot's five leaves four for a
# migration run, a psql session or the SQL editor; five each left nothing for
# the migration run that has to finish before the new copy can listen, and the
# 13 Oct 2026 deploy failed on exactly that.
#
# 10 here and 20 on the bot is 30 against 15, which is what emptied the
# dashboard on 12 Oct 2026: the pooler refuses with EMAXCONNSESSION, and that
# hits every query -- including the one that loads the signed-in user -- so the
# whole dashboard goes blank rather than one page breaking. Raise it only after
# raising the project's pool size (Supabase: Settings -> Database -> Connection
# pooling), and remember a deploy doubles whatever is set here.
# PGPOOL_MAX=3

# Whether the club records what has been paid.
#
# Outstanding is derived - total minus amount_paid - so a club that records no
# payments reads as owing its whole book. A club that arrived with a migrated
# tee sheet showed exactly this: a value on all 1,867 bookings and not one
# payment against any of them, which read as EUR 4.2m outstanding and the same
# again overdue -- an artefact of the arithmetic, not a debt.
#
# Unset (the default), the Operators page, the statements and the operator
# portal show what is booked and say nothing about balances; online payment is
# refused. Nothing is deleted and nothing is zeroed - `total` is still the
# value of the booking - so TRACK_PAYMENTS=true brings the whole ledger back,
# history and all, the day payments start being recorded.
# TRACK_PAYMENTS=false

# The day the club started recording payments. Preferred over TRACK_PAYMENTS,
# because switching payments on should not hand the club a year of debt it
# never had: bookings taken before this date carry their value and no balance,
# are never chased by a payment reminder, and read as "Not tracked" rather
# than "Unpaid".
#
#   PAYMENTS_TRACKED_FROM=2026-10-15   balances from that day on
#   PAYMENTS_TRACKED_FROM=all          the whole history (same as TRACK_PAYMENTS=true)
#   unset                              falls back to TRACK_PAYMENTS
#
# An older booking joins the ledger the moment it has real payment activity of
# its own - a payment recorded, a Stripe payment, or a link sent - because
# that is a fact about the booking rather than an artefact of the arithmetic.
# PAYMENTS_TRACKED_FROM=2026-10-15

# The schema is applied by `npm run migrate` (db/migrations), deliberately and
# before deploying code that needs it. The server does NOT apply migrations at
# boot: once it is listening it checks whether any are missing and logs them,
# because applying at boot made every deploy depend on a database connection at
# the moment connections are scarcest.
#
#   MIGRATE_ON_START=check   the default: report what is missing, serve anyway
#   MIGRATE_ON_START=apply   apply pending migrations before listening, and
#                            refuse to start if one fails (the old behaviour)
#   MIGRATE_ON_START=off     do not look
# MIGRATE_ON_START=check

# Signing key for session cookies. Generate with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# Required in production — without it sessions drop on every restart.
JWT_SECRET=

# Name of the staff session cookie. Renaming it signs everybody out once.
# SESSION_COOKIE_NAME=teemail_session

PORT=3001
# development locally: production marks cookies Secure (https only), so sign-in
# would fail on http://localhost. Render sets NODE_ENV=production (render.yaml).
NODE_ENV=development

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Logging and error reporting
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# json = one JSON object per line (for log drains); anything else = readable lines.
# LOG_FORMAT=json
# debug | info | warn | error. Default info.
# LOG_LEVEL=info
# Report server errors to Sentry. Off unless set; no request data or PII is sent.
# SENTRY_DSN=https://xxxx@oXXXX.ingest.sentry.io/XXXX
# Environment name shown in Sentry. Defaults to NODE_ENV.
# SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=production
# (RENDER_GIT_COMMIT is set by Render itself and used as the Sentry release.)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sample data. SEED_ON_START=true fills an empty database at boot (hosts with
# no shell); =force rebuilds the sample rows. `npm run seed` does the same by hand.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SEED_ON_START=false
# Club the sample rows belong to; defaults to the club existing users are on.
# (The SQL files in db/seeds fall back to the demo profile's club id, royal_dornoch.)
# SEED_CLUB=teemail
# Login the seed creates, and its password (otherwise a random one: `npm run seed`
# prints it; SEED_ON_START does not log it, so use "Forgot password" or set one).
# SEED_USERNAME=demo
# SEED_PASSWORD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Branding. This is the DEMO deployment, so everything here is left commented
# out and the TeeMail defaults in server/src/lib/brand.js (and
# web/src/lib/brand.js) apply. The names below are what a guest sees on email
# and what the dashboard calls itself.
#
# To serve a real club from this deployment, set them -- all of them. On a
# single-club install it is worth going further and changing the defaults in
# brand.js to that club's too, the way Dashboard_Beta does for County Louth: a
# club's own name should not be one unset variable away from being replaced by
# the vendor's in a guest's inbox.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The short name is what the dashboard calls itself; the full name is what a
# guest sees on email, where the formal name belongs.
# CLUB_NAME=TeeMail
# CLUB_FULL_NAME=TeeMail Golf Club
# CLUB_ID=teemail
# Prefix of the booking references the dashboard issues (waitlist conversions,
# imported rows without one), as the core API's club profile uses: TMG for
# TeeMail Golf Club, RDG for the Royal Dornoch demo profile. Default TMG.
# MUST match the core API's profile for the same club, or the two services will
# issue references in different formats for it.
# BOOKING_REF_PREFIX=TMG
# CLUB_LOCALE=en-GB
# CLUB_CURRENCY=EUR
# CLUB_TIMEZONE=Europe/London
# DEFAULT_COURSE=Championship Course
#
# The browser half is read at BUILD time, so these belong wherever `npm run
# build` runs, not only in the server environment -- and a VITE_ variable
# missing from the build environment cannot be corrected later by setting it
# on the running service.
# VITE_CLUB_NAME=TeeMail
# VITE_CLUB_FULL_NAME=TeeMail Golf Club
# VITE_CLUB_TAGLINE=Visitor Bookings
#
# The logo lives in web/public/logo.png and is served by the dashboard itself,
# which is the default when this is unset - no dependency on a third-party host
# staying up. Set it only to serve the logo from elsewhere. Leave the value
# empty and the sidebar falls back to the text wordmark.
# VITE_CLUB_LOGO=/logo.png
# VITE_CLUB_LOGO_ON_DARK=false
# VITE_CLUB_LOCALE=en-GB
# VITE_CLUB_CURRENCY=EUR
# VITE_CLUB_TIMEZONE=Europe/London

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Customer-journey email (Guest Emails page). Leave unset to hide the sending
# controls — the page still lists who is due.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SENDGRID_API_KEY=SG.xxxxxxxx
# FROM_EMAIL=bookings@democlub.teemail.io
# FROM_NAME=TeeMail Golf Club
# Where guests' and operators' replies go. LEAVE THIS UNSET.
#
# It defaults to FROM_EMAIL, which is the address SendGrid Inbound Parse
# delivers to the bot. Guest email says "just reply to this email", and that
# reply has to land there: the bot writes it to inbound_emails and migration
# 0019 mirrors it into the Inbox, onto the booking's own thread.
#
# Setting this to the club's reservations desk instead sends every guest reply
# to a mailbox the dashboard cannot see. Nothing errors; the Inbox simply stays
# empty and a guest who replied is never answered.
# REPLY_TO_EMAIL=

# SendGrid dynamic template IDs (they start with "d-").
# SENDGRID_TEMPLATE_PRE_ARRIVAL=d-xxxxxxxx
# SENDGRID_TEMPLATE_POST_PLAY=d-xxxxxxxx

# How many days either side of the play date each campaign targets.
# PRE_ARRIVAL_DAYS=3
# POST_PLAY_DAYS=2

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Booking trigger workflow (Booking Workflow page).
#
# The lifecycle ladder every confirmed booking climbs: ask for the deposit,
# chase it, warn, cancel -- or, once the money is in, confirm at 60 days, again
# at 30, the arrival details at 10, and a thank you the day after play. See
# docs/BOOKING_TRIGGERS.md for the whole ladder, and
# server/src/lib/booking-triggers-domain.js for the rules.
#
# SINCE MIGRATION 0023 THE CLUB SETS THESE ITSELF, under Settings in the
# dashboard, per club. Everything below is the fallback a club inherits until
# it saves something, so an install configured here carries on exactly as it
# did. Three decisions, each a switch of its own:
#
#   TRIGGER_WORKFLOW            these are our deadlines. The page lists what
#                               would be sent, and a person can send it
#   TRIGGER_WORKFLOW_AUTO       ...and send it daily, unattended
#   TRIGGER_WORKFLOW_AUTO_CANCEL ...and cancel a booking whose payment never
#                               arrived, without asking
#
# One word is the deployer's alone: TRIGGER_WORKFLOW=false, SET rather than
# unset, switches the workflow off for every club and no Settings page can
# lift it. Use it on a staging copy of a live database.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TRIGGER_WORKFLOW=false
# TRIGGER_WORKFLOW_AUTO=false
# TRIGGER_WORKFLOW_AUTO_CANCEL=false

# How often the unattended pass runs, in minutes. Every rung is dated to a
# calendar day, so daily is the right cadence; shorter is for a club that wants
# a same-day deposit request to go out within the hour.
# TRIGGER_SWEEP_MINUTES=1440

# The club's default deadlines, in days -- each club overrides them under
# Settings. The whole ladder is derived from these, so moving the deposit
# window moves the reminders and the cancellation with it.
# TRIGGER_DEPOSIT_DAYS=7            days from booking to secure the tee time
# TRIGGER_DEPOSIT_REMINDER_DAY=5    the first chase, inside that window
# TRIGGER_DEPOSIT_GRACE_DAYS=3      days after it closes before cancellation
# TRIGGER_BALANCE_NOTICE_DAYS=70    days before play the balance notice goes
# TRIGGER_BALANCE_DEADLINE_DAYS=60  days before play the balance must be in
# TRIGGER_CONFIRMATION_DAYS=30      days before play the confirmation goes
# TRIGGER_FINAL_DETAILS_DAYS=10     days before play the final summary goes
# TRIGGER_POST_PLAY_HOURS=24        hours after the tee time for the thank you

# How many days late a step may be and still be sent. The guard that stops the
# first run after switching the workflow on from emailing a year of history its
# own deadlines; anything older is listed as missed for a person to read.
# TRIGGER_CATCH_UP_DAYS=3

# A SendGrid template per step, where the club has designed one. Every step has
# a built-in branded email, so these are all optional -- unlike the two
# campaigns above, which refuse to send without a template.
# SENDGRID_TEMPLATE_TRIGGER_DEPOSIT_REQUEST=d-xxxxxxxx
# SENDGRID_TEMPLATE_TRIGGER_DEPOSIT_REMINDER=d-xxxxxxxx
# SENDGRID_TEMPLATE_TRIGGER_DEPOSIT_FINAL_NOTICE=d-xxxxxxxx
# SENDGRID_TEMPLATE_TRIGGER_DEPOSIT_OVERDUE=d-xxxxxxxx
# SENDGRID_TEMPLATE_TRIGGER_DEPOSIT_RESCUE_48H=d-xxxxxxxx
# SENDGRID_TEMPLATE_TRIGGER_DEPOSIT_RESCUE_24H=d-xxxxxxxx
# SENDGRID_TEMPLATE_TRIGGER_DEPOSIT_LAPSED=d-xxxxxxxx
# SENDGRID_TEMPLATE_TRIGGER_DEPOSIT_RECEIPT=d-xxxxxxxx
# SENDGRID_TEMPLATE_TRIGGER_BALANCE_NOTICE=d-xxxxxxxx
# SENDGRID_TEMPLATE_TRIGGER_BALANCE_RESCUE_48H=d-xxxxxxxx
# SENDGRID_TEMPLATE_TRIGGER_BALANCE_RESCUE_24H=d-xxxxxxxx
# SENDGRID_TEMPLATE_TRIGGER_BALANCE_LAPSED=d-xxxxxxxx
# SENDGRID_TEMPLATE_TRIGGER_BOOKING_CONFIRMED=d-xxxxxxxx
# SENDGRID_TEMPLATE_TRIGGER_PRE_ARRIVAL_CONFIRMATION=d-xxxxxxxx
# SENDGRID_TEMPLATE_TRIGGER_FINAL_DETAILS=d-xxxxxxxx
# SENDGRID_TEMPLATE_TRIGGER_POST_PLAY=d-xxxxxxxx

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Club Vero (feedback). Leave unset and the emails behave exactly as they did
# before the integration existed — they just go out without a survey link.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where Club Vero is served. No trailing slash needed.
# VERO_BASE_URL=https://clubvero.example.com
#
# The key Club Vero was given for this dashboard. It is set on the Vero side as
# PARTNER_INGEST_KEYS=dornoch:<this value>.
# VERO_PARTNER_KEY=sk_xxxxxxxxxxxx
#
# The partner name that key is registered under. Must match the name on the
# left of the colon in Vero's PARTNER_INGEST_KEYS.
# Default dornoch, a leftover of the Royal Dornoch demo profile.
# VERO_PARTNER_SOURCE=dornoch
#
# The club's slug in Vero. Only needed where one Vero deployment serves several
# clubs; a single-club Vero resolves it on its own.
# VERO_SITE=teemail-golf-club
#
# Which campaigns carry a survey link. Post-play only by default — a link in a
# welcome email asks somebody to rate a round they have not played yet.
# VERO_CAMPAIGNS=post_play

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Password reset (the "Forgot your password?" link on the sign-in screen).
# The sender credentials above are shared. Without every value here the link
# is hidden rather than offered and left to fail.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The dynamic template the reset email is rendered from. It must use
# {{reset_url}} (or {{reset_link}}); {{first_name}}, {{username}},
# {{club_name}} and {{expires_in}} are also passed.
# SENDGRID_TEMPLATE_PASSWORD_RESET=d-xxxxxxxx

# Where the dashboard is served, so the emailed link is absolute. No trailing
# slash needed. PUBLIC_URL is accepted as an alias.
# PUBLIC_URL=
APP_URL=https://royal-dornoch-dashboard.onrender.com

# How long an emailed link stays usable. Default 60.
# PASSWORD_RESET_TTL_MINUTES=60

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Guests amending or cancelling their own booking. Links are signed with
# BOOKING_LINK_SECRET,
# or JWT_SECRET where that is not set — rotating either revokes every
# outstanding manage link at once. Surrounding whitespace is stripped (as the
# core API does). At boot the server logs "LINK SECRET: set (fingerprint
# xxxxxxxx)" - the core API logs the same line, and the two fingerprints must
# match or links issued by one service are refused by the other.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BOOKING_LINK_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Membership (Membership page). Enquiries to the club's membership address
# (memberships@club.teemail.io in the demo environment) are answered by the
# core API, which hosts the application and waitlist forms. Invitations sent
# from the dashboard link to those forms, so the dashboard needs:
#   - BOOKING_LINK_SECRET above, the same value as the core API's (membership
#     links are never signed with JWT_SECRET: the core API could not check them)
#   - MEMBERSHIP_FORM_BASE_URL: the core API's public base URL, no trailing
#     path - for the demo, the core API's Render URL
#     (e.g. https://<core-api-service>.onrender.com).
# Without both, the page still works but cannot invite the waitlist.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MEMBERSHIP_FORM_BASE_URL=
# The address membership emails name ("write to ...") and their Reply-To,
# unless the club sets its own contact email. Never REPLY_TO_EMAIL/FROM_EMAIL.
# Default memberships@club.teemail.io.
MEMBERSHIP_CONTACT_EMAIL=memberships@club.teemail.io

# Guests can only request a cancellation or a change; nothing takes effect until
# staff approve it on the Guest Requests page. (BOOKING_SELF_CANCEL_DAYS, which
# once let cancellations apply themselves, is no longer used.)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# User accounts (the Users page, administrators only). A new account is
# created with no password
# and emailed a one-time link, so this template is what a new colleague
# actually receives.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# It must use {{reset_url}} (or {{reset_link}}), the same as the reset
# template. It is also passed {{headline}}, {{invited_by}}, {{first_name}},
# {{username}}, {{club_name}} and {{expires_in}}.
# SENDGRID_TEMPLATE_USER_INVITE=d-xxxxxxxx

# How long an invitation stays usable. Longer than a reset by default, because
# it arrives unannounced and the reader may be away. Default 10080 (7 days).
# USER_INVITE_TTL_MINUTES=10080

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Card payment links (the "Card payment link" section of the booking drawer).
# Sending emails the guest a
# Stripe Payment Link and marks the booking's payment Pending; Stripe's webhook
# then marks it Paid (or Deposit paid for a part payment).
#
# In Stripe → Developers → Webhooks, add an endpoint:
#   URL:    https://<this dashboard>/api/stripe/webhook
#   Events: checkout.session.completed, checkout.session.async_payment_succeeded
#           (payment_intent.succeeded also works; a payment is counted once
#           whichever events are chosen)
# and paste its signing secret below. Test mode and live mode are separate in
# Stripe: each has its own endpoints AND its own signing secret, so the
# endpoint must be created in the same mode as STRIPE_SECRET_KEY. Open
# https://<this dashboard>/api/stripe/webhook in a browser to check the URL.
# Use sk_test_ keys to try it end to end.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_live_xxxxxxxx
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_xxxxxxxx

# How often (minutes) the dashboard asks Stripe about links still awaiting
# payment, so a payment lands even if the webhook never arrives. Default 2.
# APP_URL (below) is also used to check the webhook endpoint registered in Stripe.
# PAYMENT_SYNC_MINUTES=2

# Defaults to CLUB_CURRENCY (EUR).
# STRIPE_CURRENCY=EUR

# The payment link and receipt are the built-in TeeMail-branded emails. To use
# SendGrid templates instead, set both the template ids below and
# USE_SENDGRID_PAYMENT_TEMPLATES=true — the ids alone are ignored, because a
# template keeps whatever club name and logo it was written with.
# USE_SENDGRID_PAYMENT_TEMPLATES=false

# Optional. Without it a plain built-in email is sent. A template is passed
# {{payment_url}}, {{amount}}, {{first_name}}, {{guest_name}}, {{booking_id}},
# {{club_name}}, {{play_date}}, {{tee_time}}, {{course}}, {{players}} and
# {{booking_total}}. SENDGRID_API_KEY and FROM_EMAIL above are required either way.
# SENDGRID_TEMPLATE_PAYMENT_LINK=d-xxxxxxxx

# When Stripe reports a payment the guest is emailed a receipt straight away,
# the booking moves to Booked, and its pre-play clock starts. Optional; without
# it a plain built-in receipt is sent. A template is passed {{amount_received}},
# {{paid_on}}, {{payment_reference}}, {{booking_total}}, {{total_paid}},
# {{balance_due}}, {{paid_in_full}} and the booking fields listed above.
# SENDGRID_TEMPLATE_PAYMENT_RECEIPT=d-xxxxxxxx

# The pre-arrival welcome lists only bookings whose pre-play clock has started
# (a payment has been received). Set to false to list every Booked booking, as
# before — for a club that invoices after play.
# PRE_ARRIVAL_REQUIRES_PAYMENT=true

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Email branding. The payment link, the receipt and staff replies are sent in
# the same frame as the core API's guest emails: the logo on a white header
# with a teal rule, and the club's footer. The logo must be a public URL:
# EMAIL_LOGO_URL, or else this dashboard's own /logo.png under APP_URL. With
# neither, the header shows the club's name instead of a broken image.
# SendGrid templates (welcome, thank you, the booking ladder, password reset,
# invitations) are passed the same values as {{logo_url}}, {{club_name}},
# {{brand_color}}, {{accent_color}}, {{club_email}}, {{club_phone}},
# {{club_address}} and {{club_website}} - use them in the template design.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EMAIL_LOGO_URL is deliberately left unset: the logo is committed at
# server/assets/email-logo.png and is embedded in each email, so it shows even
# when the reader's client blocks remote images (most do, by default). Set this
# only to serve the logo from the club's own site instead.
# EMAIL_LOGO_URL=
#
# The footer's contact line. Unset, these fall back to the TeeMail values in
# server/src/lib/brand.js -- a real club sets its own, because a footer with no
# address on it is how an email stops looking like it came from a golf club.
# CLUB_PHONE=+44 20 7946 0000
# CLUB_ADDRESS=TeeMail Golf Club, London, United Kingdom
# CLUB_WEBSITE=https://www.teemail.io
#
# Email colours only. The dashboard's own palette is the validated one at the
# top of web/src/theme.css and is not env-driven.
#
# Email is drawn on WHITE, so these are not the on-screen values. BRAND is the
# header rule and the portal button, carrying white text. ACCENT is every link
# and the footer's contact address - text on white, so it has to clear 4.5:1
# against it. A brand colour chosen for a dark screen usually will not: County
# Louth's gold #D4AF37 manages only 2.10:1 and reads as a smudge in an email,
# so on that install it is deepened to #8A6D1F (4.90:1) at the same hue.
# Unset, these are TeeMail's teal and its deepened green.
# EMAIL_BRAND_COLOR=#1a5e58
# EMAIL_ACCENT_COLOR=#3a8030

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Booked customers, posted to another system. Leave unset and nothing about a
# booking leaves this dashboard. When it is set, every booking that reaches
# 'Booked' is POSTed as JSON — the customer and their round, no money and no
# free text (see docs/BOOKED_CUSTOMER_WEBHOOK.md).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where to post. Must be https: the body carries a name, an email address and a
# phone number. http is allowed only on localhost, for trying it out.
# BOOKED_WEBHOOK_URL=https://crm.example.com/hooks/booked
#
# The key the receiver expects. Required — an endpoint that accepts a club's
# guests from anybody who finds the URL is not somewhere to send them.
# BOOKED_WEBHOOK_KEY=whk_xxxxxxxxxxxx
#
# The header the key travels in. Default x-webhook-key; set it to Authorization
# if the receiver wants a bearer token (then set the key to "Bearer <token>").
# BOOKED_WEBHOOK_AUTH_HEADER=x-webhook-key
#
# The partner name the receiver files these customers under.
# BOOKED_WEBHOOK_SOURCE=dornoch
#
# How often to look for bookings the endpoint has not accepted yet, and how
# many to send per pass. The sweep is what covers a receiver that was down and
# bookings the core API marked Booked in its own process.
# BOOKED_WEBHOOK_MINUTES=2
# BOOKED_WEBHOOK_BATCH=25

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tests only: a disposable Postgres for the DB integration tests
# (npm test skips them when unset). Never point this at real data.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/dashboard_test

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reading inbound email (the Inbox's summary, intent and drafted reply)
#
# The Inbox has always shown these -- it even says "Claude has written a draft
# from the club's own information" -- but where no service writes them, every
# email arrives as 'other' with no summary and the club reads each one cold.
# Set this and a sweep fills them in a minute or two after mail arrives.
#
# Without the key the dashboard works exactly as before: mail still reaches
# the Inbox, it just has no summary or draft. Nothing fails.
#
# A draft is never sent. It opens in the composer for a member of staff to
# read, change and send. The model is told not to invent a price, a date or
# availability -- it has none of those -- and to leave [square bracket] gaps.
#
# PRIVACY: the guest's email is sent to Anthropic's API to be read.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
# Claude Opus 5.5 by default: a draft that goes out in the club's name is
# worth the better model, and a club's mailbox is not a high-volume feed.
# Drop to claude-haiku-5-5 if the bill says otherwise.
# INBOX_TRIAGE_MODEL=claude-opus-5-5
# INBOX_TRIAGE_EFFORT=medium
# How often to look for new mail to read. Default 2 minutes.
# INBOX_TRIAGE_MINUTES=2
